+(duration)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole
+number of seconds, like `120`, or one or more groups of an integer followed by a unit, like `2d`, `1h30m`, or
+`1d2h3m4s`. The units are `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m` (minutes), and `s` (seconds). Each unit may appear at most
+once, in that order, without spaces. Units are case-insensitive, so `45S` is the same as `45s`. Negative numbers,
+decimals, and unknown units are rejected. A `timedelta` default is passed through unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration())
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+```
+
+```{versionadded} 8.5.1
+```
+
